  "id": "Compound::DB00759 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Tetracycline, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is linked to Cyp3a4 through multiple pathways that converge on the same core mechanism. The evidence suggests that Tetracycline, through its structural similarities to other tetracycline derivatives like Lymecycline and Methacycline, binds to Cyp3a4, a key enzyme in drug metabolism. This binding is supported by the presence of a lowest common ancestor (LCA) between Tetracycline and these related compounds, indicating a shared functional or structural basis. The LCA relationships highlight that Tetracycline and its analogs share a common pharmacological profile, which may explain their ability to interact with Cyp3a4. The scores from the paths suggest a consistent and plausible relationship, though the exact mechanism of binding remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a direct interaction between Tetracycline and Cyp3a4, likely through a common binding site or structural motif, reinforcing its role in modulating drug metabolism.",
